The original does a linear scan over the large `MESG` structs on every call. This version builds a 65536-entry index table once and then answers each lookup with a single array access.

- **Same results:** the table is filled in reverse order, so when message numbers repeat, the earliest entry wins, exactly as the original loop's first match.
- **No dependence on mutable state:** the table is built only from the constant `mesgs` profile data, never from mutable globals.
- **Safe one-time setup:** it is created in a function-local static, which C++ initializes once and thread-safely.
- **Cost:** the table uses 128 KB of memory.
- **Fallback:** if there were ever 65535 or more message entries, the 16-bit index could not hold them, so the function falls back to the original linear scan.

```cpp
const Profile::MESG* Profile::GetMesg(const FIT_UINT16 num)
{
    struct Index
    {
        FIT_UINT16 idx[65536];
        Index()
        {
            for (int k = 0; k < 65536; k++)
                idx[k] = 0xFFFF;
            for (int i = MESGS - 1; i >= 0; i--)
                idx[mesgs[i].num] = (FIT_UINT16)i;
        }
    };

    if (MESGS < 0xFFFF)
    {
        static const Index table;
        const FIT_UINT16 i = table.idx[num];
        if (i == 0xFFFF)
            return NULL;
        return &mesgs[i];
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < MESGS; i++)
    {
        if (mesgs[i].num == num)
            return &mesgs[i];
    }

    return NULL;
}
```